# Commonly confused words: "except" where "accept" (take, agree to) is meant.
# From common confused-word lists; examples are ours.
# why: Accept means take or agree to. Except means apart from. They sound alike, which is why they get swapped.
expr main <([we, I, they, you, (will), would, (cannot), (can't), (don't), (do), (does), (doesn't), please, to, gladly, happily, also, now, only, still, not, (we'll), (I'll), (I'd), (we'd)] except [the, your, this, that, my, our, their, his, her, all, any, it, them, him, me, us, payment, payments, cards, card, cash, cheques, checks, returns, bookings, applications, submissions, responsibility, liability, offers, invitations, donations, an, a, credit, debit, visa, mastercard, (Visa), (Mastercard), (PayPal)]), except>

let message "Did you mean `accept` (take, agree to)? `Except` means \"apart from\"."
let description "Fixes `except` where `accept` is meant: `We except credit cards` → `We accept credit cards`."
let kind "Malapropism"
let becomes "accept"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "We except credit cards." "We accept credit cards."
test "Please except my apology." "Please accept my apology."
test "I would like to except the offer." "I would like to accept the offer."

allows "Everyone came except Tom."
allows "We are open every day except Sunday."
allows "We accept credit cards."
allows "I like all fruit except the bananas."
